# Provenance for the OG card's three static font instances (#34, ADR-0054
# decision 12). Committing a binary is exactly the path that sidesteps pnpm's
# `minimumReleaseAge` and `trustPolicy` gates, a binary is un-reviewable in a
# diff, and these bytes feed a server-side OpenType parser on an
# unauthenticated public path. So every face records where it came from and
# how, and `T-WEB-S202` re-computes the digests below against the files on
# disk. The digest is NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT — it compares the file
# against a line the same commit could rewrite — which is why S202 also reads
# the cut out of the binary itself (`name` ID 16, `OS/2.xAvgCharWidth`).
#
# Every face was fetched by requesting the `css2` URL below with the legacy
# user-agent, then downloading the single `format('truetype')` URL that
# stylesheet resolves to. The legacy UA is what makes Google serve a STATIC
# instance: a modern UA returns woff2, which the bundled satori refuses
# (`Unsupported OpenType signature wOF2`).
#
#   user-agent:  Mozilla/4.0 (Windows NT 6.1)
#   fetch date:  2026-08-15
#
# THE `opsz` TERM IN THE FRAUNCES URL IS LOAD-BEARING AND MUST NOT BE DROPPED.
# `css2` quantises `opsz` into eighteen buckets and serves an off-bucket or
# absent request silently: `Fraunces:wght@500` with no `opsz` term returns
# Google's 13-15pt TEXT cut, and `@30`/`@32` return the 28pt cut. All three of
# the 24, 28 and 36pt cuts are 71,648 bytes with the same `fvar` absence, the
# same family name and the same `usWeightClass`, so a one-bucket miss is
# invisible to the size and to every generic check. §7.2a of plan 040 derives
# why 36 is the right bucket; ADR-0054 decision 12 records the criterion.

